In France, voting traditionally involves a low-tech process: voters mark their chosen candidate on a pre-printed paper ballot, which they then place into a ballot box.
After officials have certified the voter, the voter places the ballot into the ballot box and signs the ballot receipt.
French electoral legislation strictly codifies these procedures.
Since 1988, the transparency of ballot boxes has been required to ensure that observers and voters can testify that no ballots were present at the beginning of the voting and that no additional ballots are added beyond those properly counted and authorized.
Observers may be sent by the candidates to witness all stages of the process. At night, votes are counted under strict supervision by authorized personnel in accordance with special regulations.
