When a small group of living organisms (a small population) becomes separated from the main population to which they belong (for example, when they move across a mountain range or river, or when they migrate to a new island where they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in a different environment than the one they previously inhabited.
This new environment has different resources and different competitors, so in order for them to become stronger competitors than previously demanded, other features or adaptations are required.
The population remains absolutely unchanged; they are just as in need of adaptation as before.
Over time, the population becomes increasingly less similar to other populations as it adapts to its new environment.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, two populations become so different in appearance that they can no longer be considered members of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome of evolution and a very important part of it.
