Using ships for transporting goods is the most efficient method for moving large quantities of people and goods across oceans.
The task of naval forces, as is customary, is to preserve your nation's ability to move people and goods, while simultaneously impeding your rival's ability to move their own people and goods.
One of the most notable examples of this was the Allied military operations in the North Atlantic during the Second World War. Americans were attempting to transport people and materials across the Atlantic Ocean to assist Britain.
At the same time, the German Navy was attempting to halt this transport movement, mainly using coastal submarines.
If the allies had not won, Germany could have conquered Britain just like the other parts of Europe.
Cattle were first domesticated around 10,000 years ago in the Zagros Mountains of Iran.
Ancient cultures and tribes began preserving them for the purpose of easily obtaining milk, wool, meat, and hides.
Sheep were usually kept in enclosures located on hills or other pastures, often tended by shepherds who resembled the more familiar shepherd to the general public, and these shepherds were frequently children or young people. These traditional livestock practices are still used today.
Railway lines were established in England as early as the 16th century.
Although the wagon roads consisted merely of parallel wooden planks, they enabled wagons to achieve greater speeds and to haul heavier loads than the slightly uneven roads of those days.
Railway tracks were discovered very early. However, it took a long time for people to realize that if iron were placed on top of them, railway tracks would become even more useful.
This common practice led to increased wear of the wooden wheels of rail cars.
Eventually, iron wheels replaced wooden ones. In 1767, the first complete iron rails were produced.
