Many rhythms observed in physiology and behavior are ultimately linked to the presence of endogenous cycles and their generation through biological clocks.
Periodic rhythms that are not simple responses to external periodic signals have been documented in many living organisms, including bacteria, yeast, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are autonomous oscillators that continue their rhythmic activity even in the absence of external signals.
The fact that DNA is genetic material was one of the main hypotheses of the Hershey and Chase experiment.
Hershey and Chase used bacteriophages or viruses to transfer their DNA into bacteria.
They labeled both the DNA in the bacteriophage and the protein in the bacteriophage with radioactive phosphorus and radioactive sulfur, respectively, and carried out two experiments.
