In general, when managers begin to lead their former colleagues, two types of behavior may emerge. One end of the spectrum is trying to come across as "one of the guys" (or "one of the girls").
Such managers struggle with making non-emotional decisions, enforcing discipline, evaluating work quality, assigning tasks, and holding people accountable.
At the other end of the spectrum, a person becomes an unrecognizable individual who believes that the entire world should be changed and made their own, as if everything people have done so far should be altered.
In the meantime, the leading team is responsible for both its success and failure.
Such behavior often leads to disagreements between leaders and other team members.
Virtual teams adhere to the same standards of excellence as traditional teams, but there are subtle differences.
Virtual group members often serve as contact points for their physical groups.
Since their teams may meet at different times due to varying time zones that might be difficult for local governance to understand, they often have more autonomy than traditional teams.
The existence of a true "invisible team" (Larson and LaFasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a distinctive component of a virtual team.
"Shadow team" is a governing body composed of individual members who report to it. The shadow team establishes standards for each member.
