Excursions are an important part of any lesson. In many cases, teachers would like to take their students on trips to places that are inaccessible by bus.
Technology offers virtual field trips to locations. Students can view museum artifacts, visit an aquarium, or enjoy fine art without leaving their classroom.
Conducting excursions virtually is an excellent way to discuss travel and share experiences with future lessons.
For example, students at Bennett High School in North Carolina create a website each year about their trips to the state capital, the website is redesigned each year, but older versions are preserved online to serve as an album.
Blogs also help improve students' writing skills. Although students often begin their blog experience with careless grammar and spelling, the awareness of an audience usually leads them to correct this.
Since students are often the most critical audience, bloggers strive to improve what they write in order to avoid criticism.
At the same time, blogging "compels students to become more knowledgeable about the world around them." The need to satisfy public interest motivates students to be insightful and interesting (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is an inspiring collaborative tool and encourages students to expand their knowledge beyond traditional classroom learning.
Proper use of blogs gives students the opportunity to become better analytical and critical thinkers; by actively responding to online materials, students can establish their own positions in the context of others' writings and also express their own viewpoints on specific issues. (Oravek, 2002)
