The city of Modern Carthage was founded in the 10th century CE by the Berber fishermen, and later it was used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and Vandals, who called it Anfa.
The Portuguese rebuilt it under the name of Cascais after destroying it, abandoning it shortly after the 1755 earthquake.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city as Daru l-Badya, and it was named Casablanca by Spanish traders who established trading posts there.
Kasablanka is one of the most interesting places in all of Morocco for shopping.
It is easy to find places selling traditional Moroccan products such as clay pots, ceramic vessels, amulets, carpets, and various ornaments around Old Medina, but all of them are for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the far east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, near Rwanda.
In 2002, the city of Goma, particularly the streets in the city center, was devastated by a lava flow from the Nyiragongo volcano.
Even if Goma is sufficiently safe, any visits outside Goma should be carefully examined to understand the ongoing clashes frequently occurring in North Kivu Province.
The city is also a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along with the cheapest gorilla trekking in Africa.
You can use a moto-taxi (boda-boda) to go around Goma. For short trips, the regular (local) fare is approximately 500 Congolese francs.
Along with its relatively limited accessibility, "Timbuktu" began to be used as a metaphor for distant, exotic lands.
