Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, sharing a border with Mozambique to the east, Zimbabwe to the north, and the Limpopo River to the south.
The park covers an area of 19,500 km² and is divided into 14 ecological zones, each supporting different types of wildlife.
It is one of the main attractions in Southern Africa and is regarded as the flagship of South African National Parks (SANParks).
Scientists are working on creating a reactor capable of producing energy using the same method.
It may be beneficial to purchase a Wild Card that provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all national parks in South Africa.
Hong Kong Island gave its name to the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region and is a major destination attracting the attention of many tourists.
The cluster of buildings forming the Hong Kong skyline resembles a shiny, striped pattern due to the presence of Victoria Harbour's waters.
Go to the Kowloon waterfront, located across from the island, to see Hong Kong's best views.
A large part of Hong Kong's urban development is concentrated on reclaimed land along the northern shoreline.
This is the place that the British colonists considered their own, so if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this is a good place to start.
The Sundarbans, the world's largest mangrove forest, stretches 80 km (50 miles) from the coastal region of Bangladesh into the interior of India.
Sundarbans was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The part of the forest located in India is called "Sundarbans National Park."
However, forests are not merely mangrove swamps—they also include the last trees remaining from the vast grasslands that once covered the Indian subcontinent.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km², with approximately one-third of it being covered by water/swamp lands.
Since 1966, Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary, and it is estimated that there are currently 400 royal Bengal tigers and nearly 30,000 spotted deer in this region.
