The Oyapok River Bridge is a suspension bridge spanning the Oyapok River, connecting the Brazilian town of Oyapock with the French Guianan town of Saint-Georges-de-l'Oyapock.
The height of the two minor arches reaches 83 meters and they have two walkways, each 378 meters long and 3.5 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, and it remained closed for freight traffic until March 2017.
While the bridge's Brazilian border control checkpoints are expected to be completed, full operation is scheduled for September 2017.
The Guarani are the most significant indigenous group currently residing in the eastern region of Paraguay and have lived as semi-nomadic hunters engaged in subsistence agriculture.
In the Chaco region, local tribes such as the Guaykurú and Payagua lived, sustaining themselves through hunting, fishing, and gathering.
Paraguay, formerly known as the "Great Province of the Incas," emerged in the 16th century as a result of conflicts between Spanish conquerors and local indigenous groups.
The Spaniards began a 300-year-long period of colonization.
Since its founding in 1537, Paraguay has managed to preserve much of its original characteristics and distinctiveness.
